window.AUTH=window.AUTH||{};window.AUTH["17-340"]={"d":"17-340","id":"2018/17-340","n":"New Prime Inc. v. Oliveira","briefs":[{"k":"petitioner","label":"Brief of petitioner New Prime Inc.","date":"2018-05-14","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/17/17-340/46676/20180514134044965_No.%2017-340%20New%20Prime%20-%20Brief%20for%20Petitioner.pdf","side":"pet","n":48},{"k":"respondent","label":"Brief of respondent Dominic Oliveira","date":"2018-07-18","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/17/17-340/54725/20180718163542954_17-340.New%20Prime%20v.%20Oliveira.Respondent%20Br..pdf","side":"res","n":64},{"k":"reply","label":"Reply of petitioner New Prime, Inc.","date":"2018-08-22","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/17/17-340/60391/20180822162930717_Reply%20Brief.pdf","side":"pet","n":48}],"opinion":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/18pdf/17-340_o7kq.pdf","tr":true,"items":[{"t":"case","label":"Allied-Bruce Terminix Cos. v. Dobson","cite":"513 U.S. 272","y":1995,"id":"1994/93-1001","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the Federal Arbitration Act applied to all disputes involving commerce, and thus the arbitration clause was valid and enforceable.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"27, 30, 32","1":"47, 48, 50"}},{"t":"case","label":"Am. Trucking Ass’n v. United States","cite":"344 U.S. 298","y":1953,"id":"scdb/1952-032","sc":1,"sum":"Under the Motor Carrier Act, 1935, as amended, the Interstate Commerce Commission promulgated rules governing the use by authorized motor carriers of equipment not owned by them but leased from the owners or obtained by interchange with other authorized motor carriers. These rules abolish trip-leasing and revenue-splitting with driver-owners; require written contracts, carrier inspection, control-and responsibility …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"28"}},{"t":"case","label":"Amoco Prod. Co. v. Vill. of Gambell, AK","cite":"480 U.S. 531","y":1987,"id":"1986/85-1239","sc":1,"sum":"Section 810(a) of the Alaska National Interest Lands Conservation Act (ANILCA) provides, inter alia, that, before allowing the use, occupancy, or disposition of public lands that would significantly restrict Alaskan Natives' use of those lands for subsistence, the head of the federal agency having primary jurisdiction over the lands must give notice, conduct a hearing, and determine that the restriction of …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Apollo Computer, Inc. v. Berg","cite":"886 F.2d 469","y":1989,"court":"1st Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/886/469/","by":{"0":"14","1":"22"}},{"t":"case","label":"AT&T Mobility LLC v. Concepcion","cite":"563 U.S. 333","y":2011,"id":"2010/09-893","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held the Federal Arbitration Act preempts state laws that \"stand as obstacles to the FAA's objectives.\"","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"7, 10, 29","2":"4"}},{"t":"case","label":"AT&T Techs., Inc. v. Commc’ns Workers of Am.","cite":"475 U.S. 643","y":1986,"id":"1985/84-1913","sc":1,"sum":"The issue whether, because of express exclusion or other evidence, the dispute over interpretation of Article 20 was subject to the arbitration clause, should have been decided by the District Court and reviewed by the Court of Appeals, and should not have been referred to the arbitrator.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"7, 10, 12, 31"}},{"t":"case","label":"Atl. Cleaners & Dyers v. United States","cite":"286 U.S. 427","y":1932,"id":"scdb/1931-075","sc":1,"sum":"1. The presumption that identical words used in d.fferent parts of the same statute are intended to have the same meaning is not conclusive. Where the subject matter to which the words refer is Argument for Appellants. not the same, or the conditions are different, or the scope of the legislative power exercised in one case is broader than that exercised in another, the meaning well may vary to meet the purposes of …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"23","2":"9"}},{"t":"case","label":"Atl. Transp. Co. v. Coneys","cite":"82 F. 177","y":1897,"court":"2d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F./82/177/","op":1,"by":{"0":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bernhardt v. Polygraphic Co. of Am.","cite":"350 U.S. 198","y":1956,"id":"1955/49","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The judgment of the Court of Appeals is reversed and the cause is remanded to the District Court.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"30, 31","1":"15"}},{"t":"case","label":"Boswell v. Laird","cite":"8 Cal. 469","y":1857,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Cal./8/469/","by":{"0":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Buckeye Check Cashing, Inc. v. Cardegna","cite":"546 U.S. 440","y":2006,"id":"2005/04-1264","sc":1,"sum":"The 7-1 majority (Justice Samuel Alito not participating) ruled that challenges to the legality of a contract as a whole must be argued before the arbitrator rather than a court.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"9, 13"}},{"t":"case","label":"C&L Enters., Inc. v. Citizen Band Potawatomi Indian Tribe of Okla.","cite":"532 U.S. 411","y":2001,"id":"2000/00-292","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court held that \"under the agreement the Tribe proposed and signed, the Tribe clearly consented to arbitration and to the enforcement of arbitral awards in Oklahoma state court; the Tribe thereby waived its sovereign immunity from C & L's suit.\" Justice Ginsburg wrote for the Court that \"the Tribe agreed, by express contract, to adhere to certain dispute resolution procedures.\".","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"12"}},{"t":"case","label":"Chamber of Commerce of U.S. of Am. v. U.S. Dep’t of Labor","cite":"885 F.3d 360","y":2018,"court":"5th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/885/360/","by":{"0":"24"}},{"t":"case","label":"Circuit City Stores, Inc. v. Adams","cite":"532 U.S. 105","y":2001,"id":"2000/99-1379","sc":1,"op":5,"ar":11,"sum":"The Court held that section 1 exemption is confined to transportation workers. \"The wording of [section 1] calls for the application of the maxim ejusdem generis, the statutory canon that 'where general words follow specific words in a statutory enumeration, the general words are construed to embrace only objects similar in nature to those objects enumerated by the preceding specific words,'\" wrote Justice Kennedy.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"3, 24, 26, 27, 32","1":"passim","2":"2, 13, 18, 19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Clackamas Gastroenterology Assocs., P.C. v. Wells","cite":"538 U.S. 440","y":2003,"id":"2002/01-1435","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the common-law element of control is the principal guidepost to be followed in deciding whether the four director-shareholder physicians in this case should be counted as employees and listed six factors that are relevant to such a decision. \"Because the District Court's findings appear to weigh in favor of concluding that the four physicians are not clinic employees, but evidence in the record may contradict those findings or support a contrary conclusion,\" the Court remanded the case for a determination under the new standard.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"33","2":"21"}},{"t":"case","label":"Contec Corp. v. Remote Sol’n, Co.","cite":"398 F.3d 205","y":2005,"court":"2d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/398/205/","by":{"0":"14","1":"22"}},{"t":"case","label":"DirecTV, Inc. v. Imburgia","cite":"136 S. Ct. 463","y":2015,"id":"2015/14-462","sc":1,"sum":"The Court determined that the Federal Arbitration Act preempted state law and required the enforcement of a contract’s arbitration agreement.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"7"}},{"t":"case","label":"Edwards v. Doordash, Inc., ___ F.3d ___","cite":"2018 WL 1954090","y":2018,"court":"5th Cir. Apr. 25","by":{"0":"13"}},{"t":"case","label":"FedEx Home Delivery v. NLRB","cite":"563 F.3d 492","y":2009,"court":"D.C. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/563/492/","ar":3,"by":{"0":"32"}},{"t":"case","label":"First Options of Chi., Inc. v. Kaplan","cite":"514 U.S. 938","y":1995,"id":"1994/94-560","sc":1,"op":3,"ar":6,"sum":"The Court held that the arbitrability of the Kaplan/First Options dispute was subject to independent review by the courts and that, rather than a special abuse of discretion standard, courts of appeals should apply ordinary standards when reviewing district court decisions upholding arbitration awards. \"We conclude that, because the Kaplans did not clearly agree to submit the question of arbitrability to arbitration, the Court of Appeals was correct in finding that the arbitrability of the Kaplan/First Options dispute was subject to independent review by the courts,\" wrote Justice Breyer for the Court.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"10","2":"3"}},{"t":"case","label":"Gilmer v. Interstate/Johnson Lane Corp.","cite":"500 U.S. 20","y":1991,"id":"1990/90-18","sc":1,"sum":"An ADEA claim can be subjected to compulsory arbitration.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"9, 27"}},{"t":"case","label":"Green v. SuperShuttle Int’l, Inc.","cite":"653 F.3d 766","y":2011,"court":"8th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/653/766/","by":{"0":"6"}},{"t":"case","label":"Kindred Nursing Ctrs. Ltd. P’ship v. Clark","cite":"137 S. Ct. 1421","y":2017,"id":"2016/16-32","sc":1,"ar":2,"sum":"The Court held that the Federal Arbitration Act (FAA) preempts Kentucky state-law precedent that requires a clear statement in the power of attorney agreement in order to grant an attorney-in-fact the power to bind her principal to an arbitration agreement.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"7, 14, 15","1":"22","2":"5"}},{"t":"case","label":"Marx v. Gen. Revenue Corp.","cite":"568 U.S. 371","y":2013,"id":"2012/11-1175","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure awards costs to the prevailing party unless a statute explicitly states otherwise.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"31"}},{"t":"case","label":"Mitsubishi Motors Corp. v. Soler Chrysler-Plymouth, Inc.","cite":"473 U.S. 614","y":1985,"id":"1984/83-1569","sc":1,"sum":"The Supreme Court held that claims arising under the Sherman Act and encompassed in a valid arbitration clause in an international commercial transaction are arbitrable under the federal Arbitration Act. Justice John Paul Stevens dissented, arguing that a fair construction of the arbitration clause does not encompass antitrust claims.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"10","2":"5"}},{"t":"case","label":"Moses H. Cone Mem’l Hosp. v. Mercury Constr. Corp.","cite":"460 U.S. 1","y":1983,"id":"1982/81-1203","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"Petitioner, a hospital located in North Carolina, entered into a contract with respondent contractor, an Alabama corporation, for construction of additions to the hospital building. Contract disputes were to be initially referred to the architect who was hired to design and oversee the construction project. Disputes decided by the architect or not decided within a specified time could be submitted to binding …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"10","1":"20","2":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Nationwide Mutual Insurance Co. v. Darden","cite":"503 U.S. 318","y":1992,"id":"1991/90-1802","sc":1,"sum":"Contracts between petitioners Nationwide Mutual Insurance Co. et al. and respondent Darden provided, among other things, that Darden would sell only Nationwide policies, that Nationwide would enroll him in a company retirement plan for agents, and that he would forfeit his entitlement to plan benefits if, within a year of his termination and 25 miles of his prior business location, he sold insurance for Nationwide's …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"22, 23, 33","1":"46, 55","2":"15"}},{"t":"case","label":"NLRB v. Hearst Pubs.","cite":"322 U.S. 111","y":1944,"id":"scdb/1943-041","sc":1,"sum":"1. The meaning of the term \"employee\" in the National Labor Relations Act is to be determined not exclusively by reference to common-law standards, local law, or legal classifications made for other purposes, but with regard also to the history, context and purposes 12 Congress has shown its ability in other statutes to make clear an intent to include materialmen within the meaning of the word \"subcontractor.\" See § …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"NLRB v. United Ins. Co. of Am.","cite":"390 U.S. 254","y":1968,"id":"1967/178","sc":1,"sum":"The NLRB's determination that the agents were company employees and not independent contractors represented a choice between two fairly conflicting views, and its order should have been enforced by the Court of Appeals. Pp. 256-260. 371 F. 2d 316, reversed.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"32"}},{"t":"case","label":"Nyback v. Champagne Labor Co.","cite":"109 F. 732","y":1901,"court":"7th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F./109/732/","op":1,"by":{"0":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"O’Hare Truck Serv., Inc. v. City of Northlake","cite":"518 U.S. 712","y":1996,"id":"1995/95-191","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held, in an opinion by Justice Anthony Kennedy, that independent contractors, such as O'Hare Truck Service, are entitled to the same First Amendment protections as those afforded to government employees.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"Park ‘N Fly, Inc. v. Dollar Park and Fly, Inc.","cite":"469 U.S. 189","y":1985,"id":"1984/83-1132","sc":1,"sum":"The holder of a registered mark may rely on incontestability to enjoin infringement, and an infringement action may not be defended on the grounds that the mark is merely descriptive.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"Performance Team Freight Sys., Inc. v. Aleman","cite":"241 Cal. App. 4th 1233","y":2015,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Cal.%20App.%204th/241/1233/","by":{"0":"7"}},{"t":"case","label":"Portland Gen. Elec. Co. v. Liberty Mut. Ins. Co.","cite":"862 F.3d 981","y":2017,"court":"9th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/862/981/","by":{"0":"13"}},{"t":"case","label":"Prima Paint Corp. v. Flood & Conklin Mfg. Co.","cite":"388 U.S. 395","y":1967,"id":"1966/343","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"Noting that certain “categories of contracts otherwise within the Arbitration Act” are excluded under Section 1.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"30","1":"15, 16, 20","2":"5"}},{"t":"case","label":"Rent-A-Center, W., Inc. v. Jackson","cite":"561 U.S. 63","y":2010,"id":"2009/09-497","sc":1,"op":1,"ar":3,"sum":"The Court held that district courts are required in all cases to determine whether an arbitration agreement, subject to the Federal Arbitration Act, is unconscionable.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"7, 10, 11, 13, 14, 15","1":"16, 18, 20, 21","2":"3"}},{"t":"case","label":"Shearson/Am. Exp., Inc. v. McMahon","cite":"482 U.S. 220","y":1987,"id":"1986/86-44","sc":1,"sum":"Respondents were customers of petitioner Shearson/American Express Inc. (Shearson), a brokerage firm registered with the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC), under customer agreements providing for arbitration of any controversy relating to their accounts. Respondents filed suit in Federal District Court against Shearson and its representative (also a petitioner here) who handled their accounts, alleging …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"10"}},{"t":"case","label":"Swift & Co. v. Bowling","cite":"293 F. 279","y":1923,"court":"4th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F./293/279/","by":{"0":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Thompson Caldwell Constr. Co. v. Young","cite":"294 F. 145","y":1923,"court":"4th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F./294/145/","by":{"0":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Transamerican Freight Lines, Inc. v. Brada Miller Freight Sys.","cite":"423 U.S. 28","y":1975,"id":"1975/74-54","sc":1,"sum":"a case in which the Court held that the indemnification provision did not contravene ICC regulations designed to prevent motor carriers from sharing operating authority in order to evade safety requirements and limit each carrier’s liability to the public","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"28"}},{"t":"case","label":"Underwood Contracting Corp. v. Davies","cite":"287 F. 776","y":1923,"court":"5th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F./287/776/","by":{"0":"20"}},{"t":"usc","label":"9 U.S.C. § 1","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/9/1","stext":{"head":"§1. \"Maritime transactions\" and \"commerce\" defined; exceptions to operation of title","paras":[[0,"\"Maritime transactions\", as herein defined, means charter parties, bills of lading of water carriers, agreements relating to wharfage, supplies furnished vessels or repairs to vessels, collisions, or any other matters in foreign commerce which, if the subject of controversy, would be embraced within admiralty jurisdiction; \"commerce\", as herein defined, means commerce among the several States or with foreign nations, or in any Territory of the United States or in the District of Columbia, or between any such Territory and another, or between any such Territory and any State or foreign nation, or between the District of Columbia and any State or Territory or foreign nation, but nothing herein contained shall apply to contracts of employment of seamen, railroad employees, or any other class of workers engaged in foreign or interstate commerce."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/9/1?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":26,"ar":5,"head":"§1. \"Maritime transactions\" and \"commerce\" defined; exceptions to operation of title","tname":"Arbitration","by":{"0":"1, 3, 5, 16, 17, 24, 25, 30","1":"passim","2":"2, 12"}},{"t":"usc","label":"9 U.S.C. § 2","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/9/2","stext":{"head":"§2. Validity, irrevocability, and enforcement of agreements to arbitrate","paras":[[0,"A written provision in any maritime transaction or a contract evidencing a transaction involving commerce to settle by arbitration a controversy thereafter arising out of such contract or transaction, or the refusal to perform the whole or any part thereof, or an agreement in writing to submit to arbitration an existing controversy arising out of such a contract, transaction, or refusal, shall be valid, irrevocable, and enforceable, save upon such grounds as exist at law or in equity for the revocation of any contract or as otherwise provided in chapter 4."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/9/2?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":5,"head":"§2. Validity, irrevocability, and enforcement of agreements to arbitrate","tname":"Arbitration","by":{"0":"2, 13, 30","1":"passim"}},{"t":"usc","label":"9 U.S.C. § 4","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/9/4","stext":{"head":"§4. Failure to arbitrate under agreement; petition to United States court having jurisdiction for order to compel arbitration; notice and service thereof; hearing and determination","paras":[[0,"A party aggrieved by the alleged failure, neglect, or refusal of another to arbitrate under a written agreement for arbitration may petition any United States district court which, save for such agreement, would have jurisdiction under title 28, in a civil action or in admiralty of the subject matter of a suit arising out of the controversy between the parties, for an order directing that such arbitration proceed in the manner provided for in such agreement. Five days' notice in writing of such application shall be served upon the party in default. Service thereof shall be made in the manner provided by the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure. The court shall hear the parties, and upon being satisfied that the making of the agreement for arbitration or the failure to comply therewith is not in issue, the court shall make an order directing the parties to proceed to arbitration in accordance with the terms of the agreement. The hearing and proceedings, under such agreement, shall be within the district in which the petition for an order directing such arbitration is filed. If the making of the arbitration agreement or the failure, neglect, or refusal to perform the same be in issue, the court shall proceed summarily to the trial thereof. If no jury trial be demanded by the party alleged to be in default, or if the matter in dispute is within admiralty jurisdiction, the court shall hear and determine such issue. Where such an issue is raised, the party alleged to be in default may, except in cases of admiralty, on or before the return day of the notice of application, demand a jury trial of such issue, and upon such demand the court shall make an order referring the issue or issues to a jury in the manner provided by the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure, or may specially call a jury for that purpose. If the jury find that no agreement in writing for arbitration was made or that there is no default in proceeding thereunder, the proceeding shall be dismissed. If the jury find that an agreement for arbitration was made in writing and that there is a default in proceeding thereunder, the court shall make an order summarily directing the parties to proceed with the arbitration in accordance with the terms thereof."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/9/4?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":1,"ar":2,"head":"§4. Failure to arbitrate under agreement; petition to United States court having jurisdiction for order to compel arbitration; notice and service thereof; hearing and determination","tname":"Arbitration","by":{"0":"16","1":"18"}},{"t":"usc","label":"9 U.S.C. § 16","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/9/16","by":{"0":"6"}},{"t":"usc","label":"45 U.S.C. § 51","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/45/51","stext":{"head":"§51. Liability of common carriers by railroad, in interstate or foreign commerce, for injuries to employees from negligence; employee defined","paras":[[0,"Every common carrier by railroad while engaging in commerce between any of the several States or Territories, or between any of the States and Territories, or between the District of Columbia and any of the States or Territories, or between the District of Columbia or any of the States or Territories and any foreign nation or nations, shall be liable in damages to any person suffering injury while he is employed by such carrier in such commerce, or, in case of the death of such employee, to his or her personal representative, for the benefit of the surviving widow or husband and children of such employee; and, if none, then of such employee's parents; and, if none, then of the next of kin dependent upon such employee, for such injury or death resulting in whole or in part from the negligence of any of the officers, agents, or employees of such carrier, or by reason of any defect or insufficiency, due to its negligence, in its cars, engines, appliances, machinery, track, roadbed, works, boats, wharves, or other equipment."],[0,"Any employee of a carrier, any part of whose duties as such employee shall be the furtherance of interstate or foreign commerce; or shall, in any way directly or closely and substantially, affect such commerce as above set forth shall, for the purposes of this chapter, be considered as being employed by such carrier in such commerce and shall be considered as entitled to the benefits of this chapter."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/45/51?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":1,"head":"§51. Liability of common carriers by railroad, in interstate or foreign commerce, for injuries to employees from negligence; employee defined","tname":"Railroads","by":{"0":"26"}},{"t":"usc","label":"46 U.S.C. § 30104","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/46/30104","stext":{"head":"§30104. Personal injury to or death of seamen","paras":[[0,"(a) In General.—A seaman injured in the course of employment or, if the seaman dies from the injury, the personal representative of the seaman may elect to bring a civil action at law, with the right of trial by jury, against the employer. Laws of the United States regulating recovery for personal injury to, or death of, a railway employee apply to an action under this section."],[0,"(b) Limitation on Recovery by Aquaculture Workers.—"],[1,"(1) In general.—For purposes of subsection (a), the term \"seaman\" does not include an individual who—"],[2,"(A) is an aquaculture worker if State workers' compensation is available to such individual; and"],[2,"(B) was, at the time of injury, engaged in aquaculture in a place where such individual had lawful access."],[1,"(2) Aquaculture worker defined.—In this subsection, the term \"aquaculture worker\" means an individual who—"],[2,"(A) is employed by a commercial enterprise that is involved in the controlled cultivation and harvest of aquatic plants and animals, including—"],[3,"(i) the cleaning, processing, or canning of fish and fish products;"],[3,"(ii) the cultivation and harvesting of shellfish; and"],[3,"(iii) the controlled growing and harvesting of other aquatic species;"],[2,"(B) does not hold a license issued under section 7101(c); and"],[2,"(C) is not required to hold a merchant mariner credential under part F of subtitle II."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/46/30104?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"head":"§30104. Personal injury to or death of seamen","tname":"Shipping","by":{"0":"26","2":"14"}},{"t":"usc","label":"45 U.S.C. § 151","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/45/151","stext":{"head":"§151. Definitions; short title","paras":[[0,"When used in this chapter and for the purposes of this chapter—"],[0,"First. The term \"carrier\" includes any railroad subject to the jurisdiction of the Surface Transportation Board, any express company that would have been subject to subtitle IV of title 49, as of December 31, 1995,,1 and any company which is directly or indirectly owned or controlled by or under common control with any carrier by railroad and which operates any equipment or facilities or performs any service (other than trucking service) in connection with the transportation, receipt, delivery, elevation, transfer in transit, refrigeration or icing, storage, and handling of property transported by railroad, and any receiver, trustee, or other individual or body, judicial or otherwise, when in the possession of the business of any such \"carrier\": Provided, however, That the term \"carrier\" shall not include any street, interurban, or suburban electric railway, unless such railway is operating as a part of a general steam-railroad system of transportation, but shall not exclude any part of the general steam-railroad system of transportation now or hereafter operated by any other motive power. The Surface Transportation Board is authorized and directed upon request of the Mediation Board or upon complaint of any party interested to determine after hearing whether any line operated by electric power falls within the terms of this proviso. The term \"carrier\" shall not include any company by reason of its being engaged in the mining of coal, the supplying of coal to a carrier where delivery is not beyond the mine tipple, and the operation of equipment or facilities therefor, or in any of such activities."],[0,"Second. The term \"Adjustment Board\" means the National Railroad Adjustment Board created by this chapter."],[0,"Third. The term \"Mediation Board\" means the National Mediation Board created by this chapter."],[0,"Fourth. The term \"commerce\" means commerce among the several States or between any State, Territory, or the District of Columbia and any foreign nation, or between any Territory or the District of Columbia and any State, or between any Territory and any other Territory, or between any Territory and the District of Columbia, or within any Territory or the District of Columbia, or between points in the same State but through any other State or any Territory or the District of Columbia or any foreign nation."],[0,"Fifth. The term \"employee\" as used herein includes every person in the service of a carrier (subject to its continuing authority to supervise and direct the manner of rendition of his service) who performs any work defined as that of an employee or subordinate official in the orders of the Surface Transportation Board now in effect, and as the same may be amended or interpreted by orders hereafter entered by the Board pursuant to the authority which is conferred upon it to enter orders amending or interpreting such existing orders: Provided, however, That no occupational classification made by order of the Surface Transportation Board shall be construed to define the crafts according to which railway employees may be organized by their voluntary action, nor shall the jurisdiction or powers of such employee organizations be regarded as in any way limited or defined by the provisions of this chapter or by the orders of the Board."],[0,"The term \"employee\" shall not include any individual while such individual is engaged in the physical operations consisting of the mining of coal, the preparation of coal, the handling (other than movement by rail with standard railroad locomotives) of coal not beyond the mine tipple, or the loading of coal at the tipple."],[0,"Sixth. The term \"representative\" means any person or persons, labor union, organization, or corporation designated either by a carrier or group of carriers or by its or their employees, to act for it or them."],[0,"Seventh. The term \"district court\" includes the United States District Court for the District of Columbia; and the term \"court of appeals\" includes the United States Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia."],[0,"This chapter may be cited as the \"Railway Labor Act.\""]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/45/151?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"head":"§151. Definitions; short title","tname":"Railroads","by":{"0":"25","2":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"Allan v. State S.S. Co.","cite":"132 N.Y. 91","y":1892,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/N.Y./132/91/","op":1,"by":{"1":"40","2":"14"}},{"t":"case","label":"Arthur v. Tex. & P. Ry. Co.","cite":"204 U.S. 505","y":1907,"id":"scdb/1906-167","sc":1,"sum":"Cau v. Texas & Pacific By. Co., 194 U. S. 427, followed as to binding effect of agreements in bills of lading exempting carrier from fire loss-and claimed to have been forced on the shipper under duress and without consideration.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"34"}},{"t":"case","label":"Calhoun v. Massie","cite":"253 U.S. 170","y":1920,"id":"scdb/1919-080","sc":1,"sum":"An agreement that the fee of an attorney for successfully prosecuting a claim against the United States shall be a lien upon any warrant that may be issued in payment of the claim is void under Rev. Stats., §3477. P.175.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"26, 30"}},{"t":"case","label":"Cleveland v. United States","cite":"329 U.S. 14","y":1946,"id":"scdb/1946-002","sc":1,"sum":"1. It is a violation of the Mann Act, 36 Stat. 825, 18 U. S. \"C.§ 398, for.a man to transport a woman across state lines for the purpose of making her his plural wife or cohabiting with her as such-notwithstanding the fact that. the practice is founded on his religious belief. Pp. 16120.\" 2. While' the Act was aimed primarily at the use of interstate com.merce for the conduct of commercialized prostitution, it is …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"42"}},{"t":"case","label":"CompuCredit Corp. v. Greenwood","cite":"565 U.S. 95","y":2012,"id":"2011/10-948","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the Credit Repair Organizations Act does not give credit card users the right to bring a suit against their card companies in court.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"18","2":"5"}},{"t":"case","label":"Consaul v. Cummings","cite":"222 U.S. 262","y":1911,"id":"scdb/1911-019","sc":1,"sum":"The law implies equality between partners and does not favor claims of the survivor for services rendered after dissolution of the firm and which lead to efforts to prove disparity.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"CSX Transp., Inc. v. Ala. Dep’t of Revenue","cite":"562 U.S. 277","y":2011,"id":"2010/09-520","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that a railroad company could invoke the Railroad Revitalization and Regulatory Reform Act to challenge sales and use taxes that apply to rail carriers, but exempt their competitors in the transportation industry.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"39, 40, 43"}},{"t":"case","label":"Diaz v. Michigan Logistics Inc.","cite":"167 F. Supp. 3d 375","y":2016,"court":"E.D.N.Y.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.%20Supp.%203d/167/375/","sum":"Holding FAA’s transportation-worker exemption was irrelevant because the plaintiffs’ claims were “subject to mandatory arbitration under New York arbitration law”.","sumSrc":"brief:1","by":{"1":"58"}},{"t":"case","label":"E.E.O.C. v. Waffle House, Inc.","cite":"534 U.S. 279","y":2002,"id":"2001/99-1823","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the arbitration agreement did not bar the EEOC from pursuing victim-specific judicial relief in an ADA enforcement action. The Court reasoned that the EECO's statutory enforcement powers unambiguously authorized the EEOC to obtain the relief that it sought in its complaint, if it could prove its case against the employer.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"19","2":"5"}},{"t":"case","label":"Encino Motorcars, LLC v. Navarro","cite":"138 S. Ct. 1134","y":2018,"id":"2017/16-1362","sc":1,"sum":"The Court decided that because service advisors at car dealerships are “salesm[e]n . . . primarily engaged in . . . servicing automobiles,” 29 U.S.C. § 213(b)(10)(A), they are exempt from the Fair Labor Standards Act's overtime-pay requirements.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"60"}},{"t":"case","label":"FCC v. AT & T Inc.","cite":"562 U.S. 397","y":2011,"id":"2010/09-1279","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that corporations do not have a right of personal privacy that would prevent the disclosure of public records handed over to federal agencies.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"36"}},{"t":"case","label":"Granite Rock Co. v. Int’l Bhd. of Teamsters","cite":"561 U.S. 287","y":2010,"id":"2009/08-1214","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that federal courts have jurisdiction to determine whether or not a collective bargaining agreement was formed between two parties when the agreement is disputed, but neither party makes a challenge to the arbitration clause contained in the agreement.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"21"}},{"t":"case","label":"Griffin v. Compass Grp. USA, Inc.","cite":"2017 WL 2829619","y":2017,"court":"E.D. Va. June 30","by":{"1":"37"}},{"t":"case","label":"Hall St. Assocs., L.L.C. v. Mattel, Inc.","cite":"552 U.S. 576","y":2008,"id":"2007/06-989","sc":1,"sum":"The Court affirmed the Ninth Circuit ruling and held that the provisions of the FAA are exclusive and cannot be expanded through contractual agreement. Writing for the majority, Justice David Souter pointed to words such as \"must\" and \"unless\" in the FAA as evidence that its provisions were intended to be mandatory and incapable of modification by the parties.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"18, 49, 59"}},{"t":"case","label":"Henson v. Santander Consumer USA Inc.","cite":"137 S. Ct. 1718","y":2017,"id":"2016/16-349","sc":1,"sum":"The Court determined that a party that purchases a debt and attempts to collect the debt for its own account is not a “debt collector” subject to the Fair Debt Collection Practices Act (FDCPA).","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"60"}},{"t":"case","label":"Holt v. Cummings","cite":"102 Pa. 212","y":1883,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Pa./102/212/","op":1,"by":{"1":"40"}},{"t":"case","label":"Homer Ramsdell Transp. Co. v. La Compagnie Generale Transatlantique","cite":"182 U.S. 406","y":1901,"id":"scdb/1900-064","sc":1,"sum":"The statutes of New York impose compulsory pilotage on foreign vessels inward and outward hound to and from the port of New York by way' of Sandy Hook.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"41"}},{"t":"case","label":"Howsam v. Dean Witter Reynolds, Inc.","cite":"537 U.S. 79","y":2002,"id":"2002/01-800","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the applicability of the NASD time-limit rule is a matter presumptively for the arbitrator. Justice Breyer reasoned that the issue did not raise a substantive question of arbitrability requiring judicial resolution.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Jackman v. Rosenbaum Co.","cite":"260 U.S. 22","y":1922,"id":"scdb/1922-007","sc":1,"sum":"263 Pa. St. 158, affirmed.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"34"}},{"t":"case","label":"Kelly v. Hanson","cite":"109 Okla. 248","y":1925,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Okla./109/248/","sum":"Describing independent contractor’s agreement as an “employment contract”.","sumSrc":"brief:1","by":{"1":"37"}},{"t":"case","label":"King v. Putnam Inv. Co.","cite":"248 U.S. 23","y":1918,"id":"scdb/1918-028","sc":1,"sum":"The contention that a contract of agency to sell real estate was void because federal lands, under homestead entry, were included, presents no federal question where the state court found they were not included and the record supports the finding.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lindsay v. McCaslin","cite":"123 Me. 197","y":1923,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Me./123/197/","op":1,"by":{"1":"28"}},{"t":"case","label":"Louisville, E. & St. LR Co. v. Wilson","cite":"138 U.S. 501","y":1891,"id":"scdb/1890-132","sc":1,"sum":"Some months after the sale of a railroad under foreclosure, and its surrender by the receiver to the corporation organized to receive it, the sale being made with a provision that the purchaser should pay all debts adjudged to be superior in equity to the deeds of trust foreclosed, an order was made giving such priority to the appellee. Held, that an appeal lay in favor of the purchaser.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Luckie v. Diamond Coal","cite":"41 Cal. App. 468","y":1919,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Cal.%20App./41/468/","by":{"1":"27"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lucky Capital Mgmt., LLC v. Miller & Martin, PLLC","cite":"2018 WL 3239281","y":2018,"court":"11th Cir. July 3","by":{"1":"37"}},{"t":"case","label":"McKenna v. Snare & Triest Co.","cite":"147 A.D. 855","y":1911,"court":"N.Y. App. Div.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/A.D./147/855/","by":{"1":"47"}},{"t":"case","label":"Nelson v. Am. Cement Plaster Co.","cite":"84 Kan. 797","y":1911,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Kan./84/797/","by":{"1":"44, 47"}},{"t":"case","label":"New Hampshire v. Maine","cite":"532 U.S. 742","y":2001,"id":"2000/130_orig","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court held that \"judicial estoppel bars New Hampshire from asserting that the Piscataqua River boundary runs along the Maine shore.\" Under the judicial estoppel doctrine, \"[w]here a party assumes a certain position in a legal proceeding, and succeeds in maintaining that position, he may not thereafter, simply because his interests have changed, assume a contrary position, especially if it be to the prejudice of the party who has acquiesced in the position formerly taken by him,\" wrote Justice Ginsburg. \"New Hampshire's claim that the Piscataqua River boundary runs along the Maine shore is clearly inconsistent with its interpretation of the words 'Middle of the River' during the 1970's litigation,\" concluded Ginsburg.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"61"}},{"t":"case","label":"Owen v. Dudley","cite":"217 U.S. 488","y":1910,"id":"scdb/1909-148","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"In this case a contract made by the attorney of record with associate.counsel for professional services to be paid out cf fee:, in anIndian litigation in the Court of Claims construed; and, although -the contract provided that in case the fees were not provided for by legislation but had to be proved each party should prove, his fee indo pendently, held, that as the attorney of record had coliooted without …","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"26, 30"}},{"t":"case","label":"Pac. Mail S.S. Co. v. Joliffe","cite":"69 U.S. 450","y":1864,"id":"scdb/1864-017","sc":1,"sum":"1. When a right has arisen upon a contract, or a transaction.in the nature of a contract authorized by statute, and has been so far perfected thdt nothing remains to be done by the party asserting it, the repeal of the statute does not affect it, or an action for its enforcement. It has become a vested right, which stands independent of the statute.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"40"}},{"t":"case","label":"Paroline v. United States","cite":"134 S. Ct. 1710","y":2014,"id":"2013/12-8561","sc":1,"sum":"The Court ruled that to recover restitution, either the government or the victim must establish a causal relationship between the defendant's conduct and the victim's harm or damages.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"39"}},{"t":"case","label":"Preston v. Ferrer","cite":"552 U.S. 346","y":2008,"id":"2007/06-1463","sc":1,"sum":"The Court disagreed with the California Labor Commissioner's assessment and allowed the arbitration to continue. Justice Ruth Bader Ginsburg, writing for an 8-1 majority, stated that the California law granting exclusive jurisdiction to the Labor Commission was superseded by the Federal Arbitration Act because the parties to this case had agreed to arbitrate any contractual disputes.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Prince v. Schwartz","cite":"180 N.Y.S. 703","y":1920,"court":"App. Div.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/N.Y.S./180/703/","by":{"1":"33"}},{"t":"case","label":"Putnam Inv. Co. v. King","cite":"96 Kan. 109","y":1915,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Kan./96/109/","by":{"1":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Robinson v. Baltimore & Ohio R.R. Co.","cite":"237 U.S. 84","y":1915,"id":"scdb/1914-063","sc":1,"sum":"In a suit for personal injuries under the Employers’ Liability Act, a contract between the plaintiff, and a third party may be admissible in evidence on the trial to show that plaintiff was not defendant’s employé even though a demurrer had been sustained to a special plea that the contract contained a release of liability.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"34"}},{"t":"case","label":"SAS Inst., Inc. v. Iancu","cite":"138 S. Ct. 1348","y":2018,"id":"2017/16-969","sc":1,"sum":"The Court decided that when the United States Patent and Trademark Office institutes an inter partes review to reconsider an already-issued patent claim, under 35 U.S.C. §§ 311-319, it must decide the patentability of all of the claims the petitioner has challenged.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"54"}},{"t":"case","label":"Star Athletica, L.L.C. v. Varsity Brands, Inc.","cite":"137 S. Ct. 1002","y":2017,"id":"2016/15-866","sc":1,"sum":"The Court determined that the test for deciding whether a feature of a “useful article” is copyrightable under the Copyright Act is whether the feature can be perceived as a two- or three-dimensional artwork that is separable from the useful article and if it would be a protectable pictorial, graphical, or sculptural work on its own.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"1"}},{"t":"case","label":"Tankersley v. Webster","cite":"116 Okla. 208","y":1925,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Okla./116/208/","op":2,"sum":"Describing the “contract of employment” of “an independent contractor”.","sumSrc":"brief:1","by":{"1":"28"}},{"t":"case","label":"Taylor v. Bemiss","cite":"110 U.S. 42","y":1884,"id":"scdb/1883-098","sc":1,"sum":"Attorney — Claims against the United States — Contingent JPee — Guardian and Wa/rd — Louisiana—Tutrix.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Thompson","cite":"28 F. Cas. 102","y":1832,"court":"C.C.D. Mass.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.%20Cas./28/102/","by":{"1":"40"}},{"t":"case","label":"Vaden v. Discover Bank","cite":"556 U.S. 49","y":2009,"id":"2008/07-773","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that a federal court may \"look through\" a Section 4 petition to determine whether the claim establishes federal subject matter jurisdiction.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"18, 49"}},{"t":"case","label":"Waldron v. Garland Pocahontas Coal Co.","cite":"89 W. Va. 426","y":1921,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/W.%20Va./89/426/","op":1,"by":{"1":"28"}},{"t":"case","label":"Ward v. DirecTV LLC","cite":"342 Ga. App. 69","y":2017,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Ga.%20App./342/69/","by":{"1":"37"}},{"t":"case","label":"Watkins v. Sedberry","cite":"261 U.S. 571","y":1923,"id":"scdb/1922-152","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"1. The amount of attorney’s fees to be charged against a bankrupt estate as an expense of administration is subject to examination and approval by the Court (Bankruptcy Act, § 62a); the trustee is not authorized to dispose of property of the estate by contract with an attorney on a contingent basis.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Weary v. Cochran","cite":"377 F.3d 522","y":2004,"court":"6th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/377/522/","by":{"1":"57"}},{"t":"case","label":"Wis. Cent. Ltd. v. United States","cite":"138 S. Ct. 2067","y":2018,"id":"2017/17-530","sc":1,"sum":"The Court decided that employee stock options are not taxable “compensation” under the Railroad Retirement Tax Act because they are not “money remuneration.”","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"passim"}},{"t":"law","label":"Pub. L. No. 96-296","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/statute/94/793","by":{"1":"59"}},{"t":"case","label":"American Federation of R.R. Workers v. Erie R.R. Co., Decision No. 1962","cite":"4 R.L.B. 615","y":1923,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/R.L.B./4/615/","by":{"1":"52"}},{"t":"case","label":"Johnston v. C.I.R.","cite":"14 B.T.A. 605","y":1928,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/B.T.A./14/605/","by":{"1":"35, 36","2":"9"}},{"t":"case","label":"Ry. Emps.’ Dep’t, A.F.L. v. Indiana Harbor Belt R.R. Co., Decision No. 982","cite":"3 R.L.B. 332","y":1922,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/R.L.B./3/332/","op":1,"sum":"Rejecting contention that “railroad employees” were limited to servants of the railroad and holding “[w]hen Congress in [the Transportation Act] speaks of railroad employees, it undoubtedly contemplates those engaged in the customary work directly contributory to the operation of the railroads”.","sumSrc":"brief:1","by":{"1":"5, 41, 42, 52"}},{"t":"case","label":"United Bhd. of Maint. of Way Emps. & Ry. Shop Laborers v. St. Louis-San Francisco Ry. Co., Decision No. 1230","cite":"3 R.L.B. 700","y":1922,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/R.L.B./3/700/","sum":"Rejecting contention that “railroad employees” were limited to servants of the railroad and holding “[w]hen Congress in [the Transportation Act] speaks of railroad employees, it undoubtedly contemplates those engaged in the customary work directly contributory to the operation of the railroads”.","sumSrc":"brief:1","by":{"1":"5, 52"}},{"t":"case","label":"Air Line Pilots Ass’n v. Miller","cite":"523 U.S. 866","y":1998,"id":"1997/97-428","sc":1,"sum":"Announced by Justice Ruth Bader Ginsburg, the Court ruled the nonunion pilots cannot be forced to go through arbitration. \"Unless they agree to the procedure, agency-fee objectors may not be required to exhaust an arbitration remedy before bringing their claims in federal court,\" wrote Ginsburg.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"4"}},{"t":"case","label":"Anderson v. State Indus. Accident Comm’n","cite":"215 P. 582","y":1923,"court":"Or.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/P./215/582/","op":1,"by":{"2":"11"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bach v. Trident Shipping Co., Inc.","cite":"708 F. Supp. 772","y":1988,"court":"E.D. La.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.%20Supp./708/772/","by":{"2":"15"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bailey v. United States","cite":"43 Ct. Cl. 353","y":1908,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Ct.%20Cl./43/353/","by":{"2":"8"}},{"t":"case","label":"Barnhart v. W. Md. Ry. Co.","cite":"128 F.2d 709","y":1942,"court":"4th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/128/709/","by":{"2":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"Brewer v. Dep’t of Labor and Indus.","cite":"254 P. 831","y":1927,"court":"Wash.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/P./254/831/","by":{"2":"11"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bullock v. BankChampaign, N.A.","cite":"569 U.S. 267","y":2013,"id":"2012/11-1518","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that debts arising out of fraud or defalcation in a fiduciary capacity are not dischargeable in bankruptcy proceedings.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"6"}},{"t":"case","label":"Caron v. Powers-Simpson Co.","cite":"104 N.W. 889","y":1905,"court":"Minn.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/N.W./104/889/","by":{"2":"9"}},{"t":"case","label":"Chamber of Commerce v. Whiting","cite":"563 U.S. 582","y":2011,"id":"2010/09-115","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that Arizona laws requiring employers to verify the employment eligibility of their employees through a federal database was not unconstitutional.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"9"}},{"t":"case","label":"Chandris, Inc. v. Latsia","cite":"515 U.S. 347","y":1995,"id":"1994/94-325","sc":1,"ar":1,"sum":"Respondent Latsis' duties as a superintendent engineer for petitioner Chandris, Inc., required him to take voyages on Chandris' ships. He lost substantial vision in one eye after a condition that he developed while on one of those voyages went untreated by a ship's doctor. Following his recuperation, he sailed to Germany on the S. S. Galileo and stayed with the ship while it was in drydock for refurbishment. …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"13, 14"}},{"t":"case","label":"Chicago & N.W. Ry. Co. v. United Transp. Union","cite":"402 U.S. 570","y":1971,"id":"1970/189","sc":1,"sum":"Petitioner railroad brought this suit (after formal procedures of the Railway Labor Act had been exhausted) to enjoin a threatened strike by respondent Union, charging that the Union had failed to perform its obligations under § 2 First of the Railway Labor Act \"to exert every reasonable effort to make and maintain agreements concerning rates of pay, rules, and working conditions.\" The Union answered that the …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"Cmty. for Creative Non-Violence v. Reid","cite":"490 U.S. 730","y":1989,"id":"1988/88-293","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the statue's preparation was not a \"work for hire\" as defined by the Act because Reid was not a CCNV employee. Instead, CCNV hired Reid as an independent contractor.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"15"}},{"t":"case","label":"Connell v. U.S. Sheet & Window Glass Co.","cite":"2 La. App. 93","y":1925,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/La.%20App./2/93/","by":{"2":"8"}},{"t":"case","label":"Coppage v. Kansas","cite":"236 U.S. 1","y":1915,"id":"1900-1940/236us1","sc":1,"op":1,"ar":5,"sum":"The law was so arbitrary as to overcome the general presumption in favor of its validity. Legitimate exercises of the state police power could restrict freedom of contract, but there was no relationship here between the statute's purpose and the state's police-power goal.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"10"}},{"t":"case","label":"Dolan v. U.S. Postal Serv.","cite":"546 U.S. 481","y":2006,"id":"2005/04-848","sc":1,"sum":"The Court ruled that the \"negligent transmission\" exception was inapplicable to Dolan's case, and therefore sovereign immunity had been waived and her case could proceed. The Court accepted Dolan's argument that the exception was only meant to encompass cases of late, missing, or incorrectly delivered mail.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"9, 12"}},{"t":"case","label":"Evans v. United Arab Shipping Co. S.A.G.","cite":"4 F.3d 207","y":1993,"court":"3d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/4/207/","by":{"2":"15"}},{"t":"case","label":"Gerradin v. United Fruit Co.","cite":"60 F.2d 927","y":1932,"court":"2d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/60/927/","by":{"2":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Gustafson v. Alloyd Co., Inc.","cite":"513 U.S. 561","y":1995,"id":"1994/93-404","sc":1,"sum":"Petitioners (collectively Gustafson), the sole shareholders of Alloyd, Inc., sold substantially all of its stock to respondents and other buyers in a private sale agreement. The purchase price included a payment reflecting an estimated increase in the company's net worth from the end of the previous year through the closing, since hard financial data were unavailable. The contract provided that if a year-end audit …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"12"}},{"t":"case","label":"Kaw Boiler Works v. Frymyer","cite":"227 P. 453","y":1924,"court":"Okla.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/P./227/453/","by":{"2":"9"}},{"t":"case","label":"King v. Burwell","cite":"135 S. Ct. 2480","y":2015,"id":"2014/14-114","sc":1,"sum":"The Court determined that the plain language of the Affordable Care Act extended tax credits to federally-created exchanges as well as those the states created.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"9"}},{"t":"case","label":"Landis v. N. Am. Co.","cite":"299 U.S. 248","y":1936,"id":"scdb/1936-018","sc":1,"sum":"(1) That to grant the stay until decision of the other case by this Court on appeal, was abuse of discretion. P. 256. (2) The question whether the stay would have been proper under the conditions which existed when it was granted, had it been granted to continue only until decision of the other case by the District Court, is a question which this Court will not decide, because the conditions have changed. P. 258. …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"4"}},{"t":"case","label":"Martin v. Dixon","cite":"241 P. 213","y":1925,"court":"Nev.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/P./241/213/","by":{"2":"8"}},{"t":"case","label":"Newland v. Bear","cite":"218 N.Y.S. 81","y":1926,"court":"N.Y. App. Div.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/N.Y.S./218/81/","op":1,"by":{"2":"10"}},{"t":"case","label":"Norfolk S. Ry. Co. v. Kirby","cite":"543 U.S. 14","y":2004,"id":"2004/02-1028","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that federal law controlled the interpretation of both bills, because they were maritime contracts and the dispute was not inherently local. The Court also held that the 11th Circuit misinterpreted the bills as not protecting Norfolk.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"1"}},{"t":"case","label":"Penn. R.R. Sys. v. U.S. R.R. Labor Bd.","cite":"261 U.S. 72","y":1923,"id":"scdb/1922-091","sc":1,"sum":"1. Under Title III, § 307, of the Transportation Act, 1920, the Railroad Labor Board has jurisdiction to hear and decide a dispute over rules and working conditions upon the application of either side, when the parties have failed to agree upon a settlement under § 301 and no adjustment board has been organized under § 302.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"Rutherford Food Corp. v. McComb","cite":"331 U.S. 722","y":1947,"id":"scdb/1946-120","sc":1,"sum":"In the circumstances of this case, the boners were employees of the operator of the slaughterhouse within the meaning of the Fair Labor Standards Act, even though they worked under a contract, owned their own tools, and were paid collectively a certain amount per hundredweight of boned beef, which pay they divided among themselves. Pp. 724-726, 729-730.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"15"}},{"t":"case","label":"Taniguchi v. Kan Pacific Saipan, Ltd.","cite":"566 U.S. 560","y":2012,"id":"2011/10-1472","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the winner of a federal-court lawsuit cannot be reimbursed by the losing party for any document translation costs under 28 U.S.C. § 1920(6).","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"9"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Rosenwasser","cite":"323 U.S. 360","y":1945,"id":"scdb/1944-130","sc":1,"sum":"The Fair Labor Standards Act of 1938 is applicable to employees compensated on a piece rate basis, and employers of such employees are subject to the criminal provisions of the Act. P. 361. Reversed. APPEAL under the Criminal Appeals Act from a judgment sustaining a demurrer as to certain counts of an information charging violations of the Fair Labor Standards Act. Mr. Ralph F. Fuchs, with whom Solicitor General …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"15"}},{"t":"case","label":"Waldron v. Garland Pocahontas Coal Co.","cite":"109 S.E. 729","y":1921,"court":"W. Va.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.E./109/729/","op":1,"by":{"2":"9"}},{"t":"case","label":"Whitman v. Am. Trucking Ass’ns","cite":"531 U.S. 457","y":2001,"id":"2000/99-1257","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the CAA properly delegated legislative power to the EPA, but that the EPA could not consider implementation costs in setting primary and secondary NAAQS.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"21"}}]};